# Prolog from Elixir

Source: [SWI-Prolog](https://github.com/SWI-Prolog/swipl-devel).

Queries are ordinary Prolog text. SWI keeps its choice points while Elixir
pulls answers. Port and NIF use the same API and term representation.
Before starting Livebook, set `SWI_ELIXIR_PATH` to the absolute project path,
install SWI and build the NIF as described in the README.

```elixir
project = System.fetch_env!("SWI_ELIXIR_PATH")
Mix.install([{:swi_elixir, path: project}])
family = """
:- module(family, [ancestor/2]).
parent(ada, grace).
parent(grace, alan).
parent(alan, emmy).
ancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Y).
ancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Z), ancestor(Z, Y).
"""
load_prolog = fn session, source ->
  path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "swi-elixir-family.pl")
  File.write!(path, source)
  try do
    SWI.load(session, path)
  after
    File.rm(path)
  end
end
```

## Own and release a session

Each demonstration uses a persistent session for its queries, with `after`
cleanup on success or error. Reevaluating a cell releases its previous work.
The tiny family program and demonstrations were written for this project.

```elixir
with_session = fn backend, fun ->
  {:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: backend)
  try do
    fun.(session)
  after
    SWI.stop(session)
  end
end
```

## Unification and variable identity

Atoms carry a tag and binary name, avoiding new BEAM atoms from arbitrary
Prolog output. Repeated variables share an ID within an answer; IDs have no
meaning across answers.

```elixir
for backend <- [:port, :nif] do
  with_session.(backend, fn session ->
    [answer] = SWI.query(session, "X=f(a, [1,2]), Y=Z") |> Enum.to_list()
    {:compound, "f", [{:atom, "a"}, [1, 2]]} = answer["X"]
    {:var, id} = answer["Y"]
    {:var, ^id} = answer["Z"]
    {backend, answer}
  end)
end
```

## Recursion and multiple solutions

The example program defines `ancestor/2` recursively. Prolog modules keep
predicate names tidy. The assertion checks expected answers independently of
backend agreement.

```elixir
answers = for backend <- [:port, :nif], into: %{} do
  rows = with_session.(backend, fn session ->
    :ok = load_prolog.(session, family)
    SWI.query(session, "family:ancestor(ada, X)") |> Enum.map(& &1["X"])
  end)
  [{:atom, "grace"}, {:atom, "alan"}, {:atom, "emmy"}] = rows
  {backend, rows}
end
true = answers.port == answers.nif
answers
```

## Early stopping and failure

`Enum.take/2` closes remaining choice points. The next query can immediately
reuse the session. `call/3` returns a Boolean result or an error tuple.

```elixir
for backend <- [:port, :nif] do
  with_session.(backend, fn session ->
    [%{"X" => {:atom, "a"}}, %{"X" => {:atom, "b"}}] =
      Enum.take(SWI.query(session, "member(X,[a,b,c])"), 2)
    {:ok, false} = SWI.call(session, "fail")
    {:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "true")
    {backend, :early_stop_and_reuse_checked}
  end)
end
```
